  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1007 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nModels previously showed a possible downturn in activity today\nbut latest HRRR guidance is now showing yet another day of more\nwidespread convection. The location of the high, available \nmoisture (, CAPE values between 800-1200 J/kg (higher in \nspots), and plenty of instability remain across the area and \nwith light steering flow, here we go again. Slow moving storms \nwill keep the threat of heavy rain in the forecast with flash \nflooding the biggest concern, especially across burn scars. \nLightning, small hail, and gusty outflow winds of 40 to 50 mph \nwill also be possible. Wednesday, a shortwave still looks to \nmove across the area and one would think that with a focus for \nlift, convection would be widespread. Not so as models are \nkeeping convection similar to that seen today. Will need to keep\nan eye on latest model runs to see how things evolve. Once the \nwave moves through, flow will become north through northwesterly\nwhich will start to bring in drier air. It will take a few days\nbefore this happens as residual moisture will allow some \nconvection Thursday and Friday but a downturn is expected. The \ncenter of high pressure sets up over Utah for the weekend \nbringing mostly dry conditions, maybe a quick shower/storm over \nthe San Juans. Of course, now with high pressure almost directly\noverhead, here comes the oppressive heat reaching 10 to 15 \ndegrees above climo.",
